-- title:
--   Weak M-regularity transfers to sequences spanning a containing ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $P$ be a commutative local Noetherian ring with maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$, and let $M$ be a finitely generated $P$-module. Let $n$ be a natural number and let $f, y : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to P$ be two families of $n$ elements of $P$. Assume: (i) the list $y_0, \dots, y_{n-1}$ is weakly $M$-regular, that is, for each index $k$ multiplication by $y_k$ is injective on $M/(y_0,\dots,y_{k-1})M$ (no condition that the quotient by the whole sequence be nonzero is imposed); (ii) every $y_k$ lies in the ideal $\mathrm{span}(f_0,\dots,f_{n-1})$ generated by the range of $f$; (iii) every $f_i$ lies in $\mathfrak m$. Then the list $f_0, \dots, f_{n-1}$ is itself weakly $M$-regular: for each $i$, multiplication by $f_i$ is injective on $M/(f_0,\dots,f_{i-1})M$. Note that the two families are required to have the same length $n$, and that no inclusion between the ideals generated by $f$ and by $y$ in the other direction is assumed.
--
--   This is the regularity-transfer lemma of local commutative algebra: over a Noetherian local ring, weak regularity on a finite module of a length-$n$ sequence inside $\mathfrak m$ depends only on the ideal it generates up to containment of such a sequence, a consequence of the Koszul-homology criterion for regularity. It is used in the freeness arguments for power-series rings (with the $y_i$ taken to be Cayley–Hamilton polynomials lying in the ideal generated by the $f_i$) and in the analysis of stalks of the Igusa scheme.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_RingTheory_Sequence_isWeaklyRegular_ofFn_of_isWeaklyRegular_ofFn_of_forall_mem_span.lean

